# Fork-owned build, Nix and devenv artefacts.
#
# **There is no upstream ignore convention to preserve here.** libjpeg-turbo's
# 3.2.0 release tarball ships no `.gitignore` at any depth — the whole tree is
# the 613 files `UPSTREAM.sha256` records, and none of them is an ignore file.
# That is stated rather than left silent because the usual rule ("preserve
# every upstream ignore convention") has nothing to act on in this package:
# nothing below is upstream's, and nothing below removes an upstream entry,
# because there are none.
#
# The one upstream-adjacent decision worth naming: upstream's own build
# produces object files, `libjpeg.a`, `libjpeg.so` and the `cjpeg`/`djpeg`/
# `jpegtran` executables in its build tree. This package never runs upstream's
# build system at all, so none of those can appear here from a `cmake`
# invocation. A stray `*.o` or `*.so` in `src/` would therefore mean something
# went wrong elsewhere, not that a build ran, which is why none of them is
# ignored: a pattern that silently swallowed one would hide that.

# zig build artefacts (fork addition).
#
# These are directories rather than single files because a normal `zig build`
# creates them, and a normal `zig build` must not dirty the tree or leak into the
# package published from it. None of them is named in build.zig.zon `.paths`,
# so an ignored directory can never become part of the package.
.zig-cache/
zig-out/
# Zig 0.16 fetches dependencies into a project-local directory. This package
# declares no dependencies, so this exists only so that a future one cannot
# commit a fetched tree.
zig-pkg/

# The consumer package builds inside this repository and has its own cache and
# output tree. It is ignored here as well as in consumer/.gitignore so a build
# run from the repository root cannot commit it by accident. These are named
# explicitly rather than by a `consumer/*` pattern, which would also swallow the
# three files `build.zig.zon` `.paths` depends on.
consumer/.zig-cache/
consumer/zig-out/
consumer/zig-pkg/

# Nix and devenv working-tree artefacts (fork addition).
#
# `nix build` writes `result` (and `result-1`, `result-2`, ... on a rebuild)
# into the directory that asked for it, and `devenv` writes its resolved
# inputs and its state beside the project. None of them is named in
# build.zig.zon `.paths`, so an ignored path can never become part of the
# package either. `devenv.lock` and `flake.lock` are deliberately *not* here:
# they are real inputs, they belong to the driver, and ignoring them would be
# the wrong way to keep a build out of a commit. Neither exists in this tree
# today, and `build.zig.zon` `.paths` does not name either — `zig fetch` hashes
# the named paths, so naming an absent lock file would fail the fetch rather
# than record an absence.
result
result-*
.devenv/
.devenv.flake.nix
.devenv.flakes/
devenv.local.nix
.direnv/
